API Structure
The backend API is available under:

/api

Authentication:

POST /api/auth/signup
POST /api/auth/login

Profile:

GET /api/profile

Mind:

GET  /api/mind
POST /api/mind

Thoughts:

GET    /api/thoughts
POST   /api/thoughts
DELETE /api/thoughts/:id

Focus:

GET  /api/focus
POST /api/focus
PATCH /api/focus/:id/complete

Filter:

GET    /api/filter
POST   /api/filter
PATCH  /api/filter/:id/toggle
DELETE /api/filter/:id

Reprogram:

GET    /api/reprogram
POST   /api/reprogram
PATCH  /api/reprogram/:id/complete
DELETE /api/reprogram/:id

All user-data endpoints require authentication.

Local Development
Prerequisites
Install:

Node.js

npm

Docker

Docker Compose

MySQL client (optional)

Verify:

node --version
npm --version
docker --version
docker compose version

Environment Variables
The backend requires environment variables for:

DB_HOST
DB_PORT
DB_USER
DB_PASSWORD
DB_NAME
JWT_SECRET
PORT

Example:

DB_HOST=mysql
DB_PORT=3306
DB_USER=mindos
DB_PASSWORD=change-me
DB_NAME=mindos
JWT_SECRET=change-this-secret
PORT=5000

Never commit real secrets to Git.

The real .env file should remain local or be managed through a secret-management system in production.

Running Without Docker
Backend
cd backend
npm install
npm start

The backend runs on:

http://localhost:5000

Frontend
Open another terminal:

cd frontend
npm install
npm start

The frontend runs on:

http://localhost:3000

Running With Docker
From the project root:

docker compose up -d --build

Check containers:

docker compose ps

Check backend logs:

docker compose logs -f backend

Check MySQL logs:

docker compose logs -f mysql

Stop the application:

docker compose down

Rebuild:

docker compose up -d --build

Backend Health Check
The backend should display:

MySQL connected successfully
Users table ready
MindOS backend running on port 5000

If the backend container repeatedly restarts, check:

docker compose logs backend

Testing JavaScript Syntax
Before rebuilding the backend, validate all controllers:

for file in backend/src/controllers/*.js; do
  echo "Checking $file"
  node --check "$file" || exit 1
done

Validate all routes:

for file in backend/src/routes/*.js; do
  echo "Checking $file"
  node --check "$file" || exit 1
done

Validate middleware:

for file in backend/src/middleware/*.js; do
  echo "Checking $file"
  node --check "$file" || exit 1
done

This helps catch JavaScript syntax errors before starting the Docker container.

Troubleshooting
Backend container keeps restarting
First check:

docker compose logs backend

If you see:

SyntaxError

validate the controllers:

for file in backend/src/controllers/*.js; do
  node --check "$file" || exit 1
done

Then validate routes:

for file in backend/src/routes/*.js; do
  node --check "$file" || exit 1
done

ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED
If the browser reports:

ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED

check whether the backend is running:

docker compose ps

Then:

docker compose logs backend

The backend must be listening on port 5000.

Route.patch() requires a callback
Example:

Route.patch() requires a callback function

This usually means the route references a controller function that is not exported.

For example:

router.patch("/:id/complete", completeReprogram);

requires:

module.exports = {
  completeReprogram
};

The function name in the route and controller export must match.

Database connection problems
Check:

docker compose logs mysql

Verify the database environment variables:

DB_HOST
DB_PORT
DB_USER
DB_PASSWORD
DB_NAME

If using Docker Compose, DB_HOST should normally be the MySQL service name rather than localhost.

